4. **Name Your Project**: Give your project a name, choose a location to save it, and click `Create`.

Once you have your project set up, you can start designing your user interface. SwiftUI is a declarative UI framework, which means you describe the structure of your UI rather than the steps to build it. Here’s a simple example of a SwiftUI view:

```swift
import SwiftUI

struct ContentView: View {
    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            Text("Hello, SwiftUI!")
                .font(.largeTitle)
                .padding()
            Button(action: {
                print("Button pressed!")
            }) {
                Text("Press Me")
                    .font(.title)
                    .padding()
            }
        }
        .padding()
    }
}

@main
struct MyApp: App {
    var body: some Scene {
        WindowGroup {
            ContentView()
        }
    }
}
```

This code creates a simple view with a text label and a button. The `VStack` is a vertical stack that arranges its children in a vertical column. The `Text` and `Button` views are used to display text and create a button, respectively. The `font` modifier is used to change the font of the text, and the `padding` modifier adds some space around the views.

To run your app, simply click the play button in the top-left corner of Xcode. Your app will launch on the simulator or a connected device.

If you need more detailed guidance or have specific questions about SwiftUI, feel free to ask!

