In LaTeX tables, you can create a new line within a cell by using the `\\` command. This command is used to start a new line within the same cell. Here's an example to illustrate:

```latex
\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline
Column 1 & Column 2 \\ \hline
Row 1, Cell 1 & \begin{tabular}{c}Row 1, Cell 2\\Row 1, Cell 2 (continued)\end{tabular} \\ \hline
Row 2, Cell 1 & Row 2, Cell 2 \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Example of a LaTeX table with a new line in a cell.}
\label{tab:example}
\end{table}
```

In this example, the `\\` command is used to start a new line within the cell `Row 1, Cell 2`. This allows you to split the content of a cell across multiple lines, which can be useful for longer text or when you need to break up content for better readability.

